# Flutter/Dart testing

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic testing rules to
`flutter_test`'s widget-test framework and `package:test`'s unit-test
conventions. Applies only to `*.dart` files.

## Layout and naming

- Unit and widget tests live under `test/`, mirroring `lib/`'s structure
  (`lib/order/order_service.dart` → `test/order/order_service_test.dart`)
  — no separate ad hoc fixture tree.
- A widget test file's `testWidgets('<behavior it verifies>', (tester)
  async { ... })` description names the observable behavior under test,
  not the widget's implementation detail — `'shows an error banner when
  submit fails'`, not `'calls setState'`.
- `group('OrderService', () { ... })` to cluster related cases for one
  class/widget, matching whatever the project's existing suite already
  does.

## Widget tests

- Build the widget under test with `tester.pumpWidget(...)`, wrapped in
  whatever ancestor widgets it actually needs to render (a `MaterialApp`/
  `CupertinoApp`, a `Localizations` scope, a state-management provider) —
  matching the app's real widget tree shape, not an arbitrary bare
  wrapper.
- Locate widgets with `find.byType`, `find.text`, `find.byKey`, or
  `find.byIcon` — prefer `find.byKey` for a widget identified by role
  rather than by its current text/type when either could plausibly change
  without the behavior changing.
- Use `tester.pump()` to advance exactly one frame (a single `setState`
  or a fixed-duration animation step) and `tester.pumpAndSettle()` when
  waiting for an animation, page transition, or async operation to fully
  finish before asserting — but do not reach for `pumpAndSettle()` by
  default for everything: a widget under a `Timer.periodic` or another
  never-ending animation makes `pumpAndSettle()` time out waiting for a
  frame that never stops changing, so a bounded `pump(Duration(...))` is
  the correct tool there instead.
- Interact with `tester.tap(finder)`, `tester.enterText(finder, '...')`,
  `tester.drag(finder, offset)`, each followed by a `pump()`/
  `pumpAndSettle()` appropriate to what the interaction triggers, before
  asserting on the result.

## Mocking the boundary, not the internals

- Mock at the external boundary a widget/class depends on — a repository,
  an HTTP client, a platform-channel wrapper — defined as an interface (an
  abstract class) the real implementation and a test double both satisfy,
  using `mocktail` (no code generation, works with sound null safety) or
  `mockito` (code-generation based) to build that double.
- Never mock a class's own internal collaborator that exists purely to
  implement its behavior (a private helper, a widget's own internal
  state) — mocking one layer too deep tests that the implementation calls
  itself in a particular way, not that the widget/class actually produces
  the right externally-observable behavior, and it breaks on every
  internal refactor even when the behavior is unchanged.
- Verify a mocked dependency's interaction (`verify(() =>
  mockRepository.fetchOrders()).called(1)`) only for calls that matter to
  the behavior under test — do not over-verify every incidental call,
  which turns the test into a change-detector for implementation details.

## Async state in widget tests

- When a widget's state depends on a `Future` (a repository call behind a
  loading spinner), pump through the states explicitly: `pump()` once to
  render the loading state, then `pump()`/`pumpAndSettle()` again after
  the mocked `Future` resolves, asserting the loading indicator is gone
  and the real content is present — asserting only on the state
  immediately after `pumpWidget()` misses the loading-to-loaded
  transition entirely.
- Never use `Future.delayed`/a real `Duration` wait inside a test to give
  a mocked async call "enough time" to resolve — control the mock's
  `Future` directly (a `Completer` the test completes explicitly, or a
  mock configured to resolve immediately) and drive time forward with
  `pump()`/`pumpAndSettle()`/`tester.pump(duration)` instead, so the test
  is deterministic rather than timing-dependent.

## Fixtures and golden tests

- `testWidgets` fixtures (sample model instances, mock repository
  responses) live in a `test/fixtures/` or per-feature helper file,
  matching whatever the project already uses — do not invent a second
  fixture convention alongside an existing one.
- A golden-image test (`matchesGoldenFile`) is opt-in per project; only
  add one where the project already has a golden-test setup and baseline
  images checked in, and regenerate baselines deliberately (`flutter test
  --update-goldens`), not as a side effect of an unrelated change.

## Running and coverage

- Run `flutter test` (or `flutter test --coverage` when the project
  tracks coverage) before reporting a testing task done; fix a failing
  test by correcting the test or the fixture it depends on, not by
  weakening its `expect`/`find` assertion to make it pass.
